<div class="view on" id="view-work">
  <header>
    <div class="brandrow">
      <span class="mark">⎈</span><h1>helm</h1>
      <div class="stats" id="stats"></div>
    </div>
    <p id="hello">your projects, remembered</p>
  </header>

  <div id="banner">helm isn’t answering — start it with <code>helm web</code>; retrying quietly…</div>

  <!-- SEPARATE from #banner on purpose: that one is a connectivity ERROR and
       this one is an advisory about the server's own age. One element would
       let either clobber the other, and the case that matters most is both
       being true at once. -->
  <div id="stalebanner"></div>

  <!-- ON YOU (the owner's merge of board, work and scheduler into one Work
       page): what is waiting on him comes first — the decisions he answers
       here, and the owner asks and owner-held land requests the fleet filed,
       off the one /api/lr read. ONE QUIET LINE when both reads say nothing is
       waiting; "not read yet" until both have answered, never a quiet zero
       over a read that has not arrived. -->
  <section id="onyou">
    <div id="onyouhead"><b>on you</b>
      <span id="onyouline"><span class="bmut">not read yet</span></span>
    </div>
    <div id="onyouasks" hidden></div>
    <!-- OWNER DECISION QUEUE: the durable "waiting on YOU" surface (the
         store review queue generalized — owner 2026-08-04: "so that I can
         just go review a durable queue"). Any seat files a card
         (`helm decide file`); you answer HERE at your leisure; the verdict
         DMs the filing seat via its beacon. Empty is a real state and
         says so. -->
    <section id="odq" hidden>
      <div id="odqhead"><b>owner decisions</b>
        <span class="cfgmut" id="odqmeta">not read yet</span>
        <button class="chip" id="odqReload" style="margin-left:auto">↻ reload</button>
      </div>
      <div id="odqlist"><div class="empty">not read yet</div></div>
    </section>
  </section>

  <!-- THE BOARD (task/2975): the lanes line, then ONE ROW PER PROJECT, light
       first, because the light is AUTHORIZATION and a family's burn colour is
       only SUPPLY. "lanes: R running of M possible" — R the live worktree
       lanes across every project, M the seats able to take work now — with
       one chip per model family in its credit colour, and a tap on it opens
       Fleet's seats. The signing pulse sits beside it, the burn flags one fold
       below. Everything boots saying "not read yet". -->
  <section id="board">
    <div id="bhead">
      <span id="bheadline"><span class="bcap bmut">lanes: not read yet</span></span>
      <span id="dreggstrip" title="the signing pulse: is activity landing on the shared signed record, and what landed last — click for the full ledger">⛓ signing — not read yet</span>
    </div>
    <details class="grp" id="flagcard">
      <summary>burn flags<span class="gmeta">how much may run right now, per model family — one fold, read not probed</span></summary>
      <div id="flagoverall"></div>
      <div id="flagrows"></div>
    </details>
    <div id="controls">
      <input id="q" type="search" placeholder="filter projects…  ( / )" autocomplete="off">
      <button class="tog" id="togRetired">retired</button>
      <span id="count"></span>
    </div>
    <div id="brows"></div>
    <div id="empty"><div class="glyph">⎈</div>no projects yet — run <code>helm sync</code>, then refresh.</div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE FLEET-WIDE KANBAN, grouped by project: every project with work in
       flight or landed lately, each as its own four columns (building,
       review, gate, landed) — the same data as each row's own kanban, off
       /api/board. The project this helm serves comes from its own pipeline
       read; every other project from the all-projects read, which runs in the
       background, so its columns say "loading" until it lands and UNKNOWN if
       it fails, never empty. Nothing is withheld here.
       Under it, folded: this helm's own land requests row by row (the wall
       pollLr renders by id — honesty marks, closed footer, board/list toggle
       unchanged), and the scheduler: who waits on whom over the same read. -->
  <section class="ltier" id="tierpipeline">
    <div class="ltierhead"><b>work landing · the kanban</b><span>every
      change on its way into the shared code — who wrote it, who reviewed
      it, and whether git agrees it landed. one row is what helm calls a
      “land request”; alarms mean the record and reality disagree.</span></div>
    <section id="fleetkb"><div class="fkline bmut">land requests not read yet</div></section>
    <details class="grp" id="walldetail">
      <summary>row by row<span class="gmeta">this helm's own land requests, every mark and verdict — the detail behind its group above</span></summary>
      <section id="lrsec"></section>
    </details>
  </section>
  <details class="grp" id="schedfold">
    <summary>who waits on whom<span class="gmeta">the scheduler: every open land request, grouped by the holder it waits on — the same read as the pipeline above</span></summary>
    <section id="schedulersec"><div class="empty">scheduler not read yet — this is not an empty graph</div></section>
  </details>

  <!-- THE TASK BACKLOG, and the three obligation lists that were beside it on
       the work tab, under the kanban: the queue behind the work in flight. -->
  <div id="worksections">
    <!-- TASK BACKLOG (#218): the section this container was BUILT for
         (docs/VERBS.md task-backlog intent; the sibling-joins assertion in
         test_web_decisions). Team tasks the fleet files — read, annotate,
         never decide-gated: the home badge counts decisions ONLY, so the
         3 cards that need the owner stay findable above the 79 that do
         not. Closed rows render as dim one-liners under a fold: a cited
         task/NNN resolving to a sentence is the point (55% of citations
         point at closed ids); resolving to nothing is the bug. -->
    <section id="otq">
      <div id="otqhead"><b>task backlog</b>
        <span class="cfgmut" id="otqmeta">not read yet</span>
        <button class="chip" id="otqReload" style="margin-left:auto">↻ reload</button>
      </div>
      <!-- THE QUEUE HEADER (task/2622), owner: "how am I supposed to help or
           cross-check the priority ordering of such a massive outstanding
           list". 477 open rows the morning he asked. He reads TOTAL NUMBERS
           in the browser pane and does not read row text, so the numbers come
           FIRST and the rows follow — five counts and the oldest open P0/P1
           age, the same line the board home's queue cell draws off the same
           function and the same read. -->
      <div id="otqqueue" class="tqhead">not read yet</div>
      <!-- THE CHIPS slice the payload already in hand; a filter that costs a
           read would blank this card while it waited, and the whole backlog
           arrives in one body anyway. Nothing here is a second API. -->
      <div id="otqchips" class="tqchips"></div>
      <div id="otqlist"><div class="empty">not read yet</div></div>
    </section>

    <!-- BURN-DOWN (task/955) — the owner asked to be ABLE TO BURN DOWN ALL
         ROWS, and until this section the answer lived only in the obligation
         ledger's own verb, which is not even on trunk. He is GUI-first; a terminal-only
         answer does not discharge a request to SEE the backlog.
         The meta line NEVER says "0 owed" for a ledger it could not read —
         that is the same absent/cannot-look triad the task backlog above
         enforces, and the reason both surfaces spell the failure out. -->
    <section id="obd">
      <div id="obdhead"><b>burn-down</b>
        <span class="cfgmut" id="obdmeta">not read yet</span>
        <button class="chip" id="obdReload" style="margin-left:auto">↻ reload</button>
      </div>
      <div id="obdlist"><div class="empty">not read yet</div></div>
    </section>

    <!-- CURED, AWAITING RE-DISPATCH — the SECOND obligation type, and the
         console was silent about it. These rows are finished work with no
         reader: the author cured and never handed back, so the board reads
         them as owed BY the author while the author believes they are done.
         Nobody is waiting on either side. They are invisible to every routine
         sweep because `dispatch list --open` excludes CURED by construction.
         Its state is INDEPENDENT of the burn-down above: two questions over
         two sources, and one going blind must not blank the other. -->
    <!-- UNDECLARED VERDICTS — the THIRD state, split out of the burn-down
         after a post-land review: cmd_owed defines an undeclared
         verdict as NEITHER owed nor clear, and the first version of this
         surface counted all 26 of them inside "owed". That asserted unknowns
         are debts. It over-reported in the SAFE direction, which is exactly
         why it would have survived unnoticed — nobody is alarmed by a backlog
         that looks too long. These rows need somebody to SAY whether a cure is
         owed; they are not a cure anybody owes yet. -->
    <section id="oub">
      <div id="oubhead"><b>undeclared verdicts</b>
        <span class="cfgmut" id="oubmeta">not read yet</span>
      </div>
      <div id="oublist"><div class="empty">not read yet</div></div>
    </section>

    <section id="ocb">
      <div id="ocbhead"><b>cured, awaiting re-dispatch</b>
        <span class="cfgmut" id="ocbmeta">not read yet</span>
      </div>
      <div id="ocblist"><div class="empty">not read yet</div></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- SYSTEM DASHBOARD, under the board: the whole system in one glance —
       record, fleet, pipeline, in-flight, recent lands, owner debt — each row the same
       shape so components COMPARE instead of stacking. It replaced a ~90-row
       pipeline wall of equal visual weight as the first paint (owner
       2026-07-28: "the homepage should be much more of an above-the-fold
       dashboard of the system and its components"; the 0.2 council found the
       same hole independently: "NO HEALTH AT A GLANCE"). The wall itself
       is the kanban above on this same page (it lived on the ledger tab
       until the owner merged the pages); the pipeline row below links
       straight to it.

       The skeleton is STATIC and each cell is owned by exactly one poll
       (record: pollLr 45s · fleet: pollChat 2s · pipeline / lands / owner:
       pollLr 45s) — no new polling, and a renderer rewriting
       the whole band would clobber the other polls' cells between beats.
       Every cell BOOTS saying "not read yet": a dashboard that cannot tell
       ZERO from UNREAD is the exact defect this console exists to kill. -->
  <section id="dash">
    <div class="dhead"><span class="dlabel">system</span>
      <span class="dhow">the record's own numbers — <code>helm lr list</code> · <code>helm chat roster</code> · <code>helm premise-check &lt;id&gt;</code></span></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="who owes the next move, from the same projection helm lr list reads: one name per holder with how many live land loops are waiting on it, longest wait first inside a tie">owed by</span>
      <div class="dval" id="downedby"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="the local attest-chain's verification (with the pipeline read, 45s); the dregg signing pulse rides the board's headline">record</span>
      <div class="dval">
        <span id="dchain" class="dmut">chain not read yet</span>
      </div></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="who is on right now — same vocabulary as chat + roster: green active <2m · orange unverified · yellow quiet <15m · gone">fleet</span>
      <div class="dval" id="dfleet"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="every open land loop, partitioned once per row: contrary > honored (verdict HONORED through succession) > stalled > unmeasurable > moving — one bar, widths are the counts">pipeline</span>
      <div class="dval" id="dpipe"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="the land-request ledger's own closed-as-landed rows, newest first — the same read helm lr list makes, so the card and the command line cannot disagree; each row names its task, its reviewed tip, its gate receipt and its age">landed</span>
      <div class="dval" id="dlands"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
    <!-- THE TASK QUEUE'S OWN NUMBERS (task/2622). The pipeline row above
         counts LAND LOOPS — work already in flight — and the owner's question
         was about the 477-row BACKLOG behind it, which had no number anywhere
         on this page. Fed by the task poll that already runs for the work
         tab's card (one poll owns one cell), and it obeys the band's law like
         every other row: it names its command-line read, shows that read's
         age, and renders NOTHING once the read is past its freshness bound. -->
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="the fleet task backlog by rank, from the same read helm task list makes: how many P0/P1/P2/P3 are live, how many are in progress, and how long the oldest open P0 and P1 have been waiting">backlog</span>
      <div class="dval" id="dqueue"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
    <div class="drow"><span class="dlab" title="what is waiting on YOU: the fleet-filed owner asks and the land requests held for your approval, both off the one /api/lr read">owner</span>
      <div class="dval" id="downer"><span class="dmut">not read yet</span></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ANSWERS FOR YOU (owner ruling, task/2364): replies that address HIM,
       from the same reading of the room the push notification path uses. It
       sits directly under the band and ABOVE readiness because it is the one
       thing on this page that is waiting on him rather than reporting to him.
       HIDDEN AT ZERO — chat states emptiness properly, and an empty card here
       is one more thing to look past. It is not an inbox: it holds nothing,
       acknowledges nothing, and reading the room clears it. -->
  <section id="danswers" hidden></section>

  <!-- AWAY MODE (task/3018): the owner's away flag and his notice to every
       agent, set here because he does not type verbs. It sits beside the
       owner row above because both are about him and the fleet. Agents learn
       a change at their next turn; nothing here wakes anyone. It boots saying
       "Not read yet", and an unreadable flag is never drawn as "here". -->
  <section id="posturesec"></section>

  <!-- READINESS: `helm ready`'s five signals — may forward work resume after
       a crash/reboot? (#163). ALWAYS visible: a readiness gauge that hides
       when unread is a gauge nobody checks after the reboot it exists for.
       It boots saying "not read yet" (the dash law: zero and unread are
       different facts). Advisory — it renders, it never gates. -->
  <section id="readysec"></section>

  <!-- GUARD REFUSALS, AND THE OWNER'S DIAL. How often a guard stopped an
       agent this week (`helm friction`'s own numbers), and the one number
       that decides when an agent is told it may stop and repair a guard. It
       sits beside readiness because both answer "is the fleet's own machinery
       getting in its way", and it is a control he turns here because he does
       not type verbs. It boots saying "Not read yet", and an unreadable
       record is never drawn as zero refusals. -->
  <section id="frictionsec"></section>

  <!-- FLEET NOTES: this is the "what happened overnight" answer the fleet
       leaves for you (`helm note set <key> <text>`). It used to live on a nav
       tab called "cave", over a tmpfs relay, so a reboot ate it. Durable now,
       and on the tab you already open. -->
  <section id="notesec"></section>
  <section id="store"></section>
  <section id="sessions"></section>
  <section id="skillsec"></section>

  <footer id="foot"></footer>
</div>

